  abstract = {Maintaining robust connectivity in intelligent unmanned cluster networks poses a significant challenge in highly dynamic and adversarial environments, where frequent node failures lead to severe network degradation and jeopardize mission reliability. Addressing the urgent need for continuous connectivity maintenance, this study proposes a topology reconstruction method based on multi-objective optimization to enhance the resilience of unmanned cluster networks. To achieve targeted topology repair, this study integrates a TOPSIS-based multi-attribute decision-making framework incorporating four topological centrality metrics—degree centrality, closeness centrality, K-shell, and eigenvector centrality—to accurately identify critical nodes and overcome the limitations of single-metric evaluations Combined with a coverage hole detection mechanism, this study constructs a cube-shaped detection region and realizes precise localization of network damage through geometric analysis of boundary intersection points, thereby reliably identifying coverage voids induced by node failures. A comprehensive multi-objective optimization model is formulated to simultaneously maximize network coverage, minimize energy consumption, and improve link stability, effectively balancing the inherent trade-offs among these objectives. The model is efficiently solved using an adaptive particle swarm optimization (APSO) algorithm, which dynamically adjusts learning factors to avoid premature convergence. E},
